================================================================================================================================

# 1. PURPOSE

================================================================================================================================

This document is the authoritative master reference for the:

**Atomic Edit Persistence**

feature.

The purpose of this feature was to eliminate the unsafe client-side:

DELETE → INSERT

pattern previously used by Manual Trade editing.

The new architecture ensures that replacing Manual Trade executions is performed as one atomic database operation.

The core safety requirement is:

```text
DELETE OLD EXECUTIONS
        +
INSERT CORRECTED EXECUTIONS

MUST SUCCEED TOGETHER.
```

If the replacement fails:

```text
ROLLBACK
```

The original execution state must remain intact.

================================================================================================================================

# 2. CORE ARCHITECTURAL PRINCIPLE

================================================================================================================================

Elite X is execution-first.

```text
Executions
    ↓
FIFO Reconstruction
    ↓
Derived Trades
```

Manual Edit therefore does NOT directly modify reconstructed trades.

It modifies the canonical executions.

After the execution change:

```text
Executions
    ↓
pairTrades()
    ↓
Derived Trade State
```

The FIFO engine remains the authoritative reconstruction engine.

================================================================================================================================

# 3. ORIGINAL EDIT PROBLEM

================================================================================================================================

The original Manual Edit persistence behavior was conceptually:

```text
EditTradeModal
      ↓
DELETE old execution(s)
      ↓
INSERT corrected execution(s)
```

These operations were separate persistence operations.

This created a dangerous failure case:

```text
DELETE succeeds
      ↓
INSERT fails
      ↓
Original data is already gone
      ↓
Corrected data does not exist
```

For a financial/trading journal, this is unacceptable.

The persistence operation therefore needed transaction-level atomicity.

================================================================================================================================

# 4. NEW ATOMIC EDIT ARCHITECTURE

================================================================================================================================

The new architecture is:

```text
EditTradeModal
      ↓
POST /api/trades/edit
      ↓
Authenticate user
      ↓
replaceManualExecutionsAtomically()
      ↓
validate input
      ↓
Supabase RPC
      ↓
DELETE + INSERT
      ↓
COMMIT or ROLLBACK
```

The client no longer performs the destructive delete/insert sequence itself.

================================================================================================================================

# 5. CLIENT RESPONSIBILITY

================================================================================================================================

Current file:

```text
components/trades/EditTradeModal.tsx
```

The client is responsible for:

* collecting edited values;
* performing user-facing validation;
* determining the appropriate edit mode;
* constructing corrected executions;
* obtaining the current authentication session;
* sending the authenticated request to the server;
* waiting for the server response;
* closing/reloading only after successful persistence.

The client is NOT the authority for database ownership or atomic persistence.

================================================================================================================================

# 6. MANUAL EDIT API

================================================================================================================================

Current file:

```text
app/api/trades/edit/route.ts
```

The API performs:

```text
Authorization header
        ↓
Bearer token
        ↓
Supabase authentication
        ↓
authenticated user
        ↓
user.id
```

The authenticated user ID is derived server-side.

The API does not trust a client-provided user ID for ownership.

The API validates that the request contains the expected executions array.

It then calls:

```text
replaceManualExecutionsAtomically()
```

The API returns success only after the server persistence operation succeeds.

================================================================================================================================

# 7. ATOMIC SERVER HELPER

================================================================================================================================

Current file:

```text
lib/server/trades/replaceManualExecutionsAtomically.ts
```

Responsibilities:

* validate replacement input;
* invoke server-side Manual Execution validation;
* serialize normalized executions into database format;
* deduplicate executions by ID;
* call the Supabase atomic RPC;
* throw if the database operation fails.

The helper does NOT:

* reconstruct trades;
* calculate P&L;
* implement FIFO;
* modify reconstructed trade records directly.

It is a persistence-layer component.

================================================================================================================================

# 8. SERVER-SIDE VALIDATION

================================================================================================================================

The atomic replacement helper now invokes:

```text
lib/server/trades/validateManualExecutions.ts
```

before the database replacement operation.

Therefore the current sequence is:

```text
Incoming executions
        ↓
Server-side validation
        ↓
Serialization
        ↓
Deduplication
        ↓
Atomic database replacement
```

This validation layer was introduced as Feature #27.

The validator is documented separately under:

```text
Server-Side Suite/
└── Server-Side Validation Mirror
```

================================================================================================================================

# 9. DATABASE RPC

================================================================================================================================

The Atomic Edit persistence operation uses:

```text
public.replace_manual_executions_atomically(...)
```

The function is configured as:

```text
SECURITY DEFINER
set search_path = public
```

The RPC performs the replacement inside PostgreSQL's transactional execution context.

Conceptually:

```text
Validate
    ↓
DELETE old execution set
    ↓
INSERT corrected execution set
    ↓
Success → COMMIT
    ↓
Failure → ROLLBACK
```

================================================================================================================================

# 10. ATOMIC GUARANTEE

================================================================================================================================

The fundamental guarantee is:

```text
OLD STATE
   ↓
ATOMIC REPLACEMENT
   ↓
┌───────────────┐
│               │
SUCCESS       FAILURE
│               │
↓               ↓
COMMIT        ROLLBACK
│               │
↓               ↓
NEW STATE     OLD STATE
```

There must never be an intended intermediate state where:

```text
OLD EXECUTIONS = deleted
NEW EXECUTIONS = not inserted
```

after an unsuccessful edit.

================================================================================================================================

# 11. EDIT MODES

================================================================================================================================

Manual Edit supports two different replacement scopes.

### PARTIAL EXIT

```text
deleteMode = "execution_id"
```

The exact selected exit execution is replaced.

This is critical for partial-exit lifecycles because deleting/replacing the entire contract lifecycle would affect unrelated exits.

### COMPLETE / OPEN POSITION LIFECYCLE

```text
deleteMode = "contract_key"
```

The lifecycle represented by the Manual contract key is replaced as a unit.

This allows the complete Manual lifecycle to be edited consistently.

================================================================================================================================

# 12. PARTIAL EXIT EDIT

================================================================================================================================

Example:

```text
BUY 100
SELL 20
SELL 20
SELL 20  ← selected exit
SELL 20
SELL 20
```

Editing the selected exit must affect only that execution.

Conceptually:

```text
Exact Exit Execution ID
        ↓
Delete exact execution
        ↓
Insert corrected exit
```

The other exits remain untouched.

The lifecycle's `contractKey` is preserved.

================================================================================================================================

# 13. COMPLETE LIFECYCLE EDIT

================================================================================================================================

Example:

```text
BUY 100
SELL 100
```

A Complete Trade edit replaces the lifecycle's executions.

The lifecycle is identified through:

```text
contractKey
```

The replacement therefore operates on the entire Manual lifecycle represented by that key.

================================================================================================================================

# 14. CONTRACT KEY

================================================================================================================================

Manual executions are identified through:

```text
MANUAL-
```

contract keys.

Example:

```text
MANUAL-AAPL-<lifecycle-id>
```

The Manual Edit persistence path uses the contract key when the edit represents the entire lifecycle.

This prevents Manual Edit from accidentally targeting unrelated broker executions.

================================================================================================================================

# 15. EXECUTION SOURCE OF TRUTH

================================================================================================================================

The edited executions remain the canonical state.

After a successful edit:

```text
Updated Executions
        ↓
pairTrades()
        ↓
Updated Derived Trade
```

There is no separate:

```text
Manual Trade Update Engine
```

and no direct reconstructed-trade mutation is required.

================================================================================================================================

# 16. FIFO PROTECTION

================================================================================================================================

Authoritative FIFO implementation:

```text
lib/parsers/pairTrades.ts
```

This file remains protected.

Atomic Edit does not implement FIFO.

Atomic Edit does not calculate derived P&L.

Atomic Edit does not manually rebuild positions.

The existing reconstruction engine remains responsible for deriving the resulting state.

================================================================================================================================

# 17. ERROR HANDLING

================================================================================================================================

If authentication fails:

```text
401 Unauthorized
```

If the request is invalid:

```text
400 Invalid request
```

If the server/database operation fails:

```text
500
```

The client only treats the edit as successful after the API returns success.

================================================================================================================================

# 18. UI SUCCESS BEHAVIOR

================================================================================================================================

The current EditTradeModal waits for the server response.

Conceptually:

```text
POST /api/trades/edit
        ↓
SUCCESS
        ↓
close modal
        ↓
reload
```

If persistence fails:

```text
API error
        ↓
do not treat edit as successful
```

This prevents the UI from claiming an edit succeeded when the database did not.

